<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jamal al-Din al-Afghani</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1838/39–<a href="/years/1897">1897</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Jamal ad-Din al-Afghani, Jamal ad-Din Asadabadi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jamal al-Din al-Afghani was a political activist and Islamic thinker who travelled across the Muslim world in the late 19th century, one of the founders of Islamic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modernism">modernism</a>. He claimed Afghan origin, but several scholars hold that he was born into a Shia family in Asadabad, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> he gained a disciple, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-abduh">Muhammad Abduh</a>, with whom he published the journal al-'Urwa al-wuthqa in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in 1884, calling for unity among Muslims. He was implicated in the plot of his follower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mirza-reza-kermani">Mirza Reza Kermani</a>, who shot Naser al-Din Shah of Iran in 1896.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jamal-al-din-al-afghani.jpg" alt="Jamal al-Din al-Afghani" width="320" height="439" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Al_afghani.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
